<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Garnier</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Marie Joseph François Garnier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Garnier was a French naval officer and explorer. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> exploration expedition of 1866–1868 after the death of its commander, Ernest Doudart de Lagrée, showing that the river was not a navigable route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Sent to Tonkin in 1873, he seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> citadel without authorisation and was killed in December 1873 in an ambush by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-flag-army">Black Flag Army</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-garnier.jpg" alt="Francis Garnier" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Sarrut (1822-????) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lieutenant_Francis_Garnier_in_the_early_1870s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Marie Joseph François Garnier was born on 25 July 1839 in Saint-Étienne, the second son of Louis-Alexandre Garnier and Anne Marie Félicité Garnier. At sixteen he joined the Ecole Navale, to the dismay of his family, who thought a military career dangerous. In early 1860, serving on the Duperré during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium War</a>, he jumped into a stormy sea at night to save a cavalry lieutenant and was promoted to ensign. He fought in the Cochinchina Campaign under Admiral Charner and in 1862 became inspector of native affairs in Cochinchina, administering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cholon">Cholon</a>, a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At Garnier's suggestion a mission was sent to explore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> valley. Garnier was thought too young to command, and authority went to Captain Ernest Doudard de Lagrée. In 1870 the Royal Geographical Society gave Garnier its Patron's Medal; of the 5,392 miles traversed from Kratié to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, 3,625 were surveyed, nearly all the observations being his. When Lagrée died he took command and led the expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the coast.</p><p>In late summer 1873 a dispute between the French trader Jean Dupuis and the Vietnamese authorities created a crisis at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>. Admiral Dupré sent Garnier, at the Vietnamese court's request, to settle it, with 180 men and two gunboats. When the mandarins refused to negotiate, he decided to take the city without orders. On 20 November his men stormed the citadel, defended by 7,000, and took it in under an hour. In two weeks the French took the whole Red River Delta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Vietnamese authorities sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yongfu">Liu Yongfu</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-flag-army">Black Flag Army</a>. On 21 December 1873 about 600 Black Flags approached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>'s west gate. Garnier led a small party out in pursuit, slipped at a dyke and was killed. The French authorities had not approved the conquest, and Paul-Louis-Félix Philastre ordered the conquered cities evacuated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saigon-1874">Treaty of Saigon</a> of 15 March 1874 recognised French sovereignty over Cochinchina and opened the Red River to French trade. In the colonial period Garnier was honoured as a martyr of the conquest of Tonkin, and two gunboats were named after him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-riviere">Henri Rivière</a> in 1884.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francis Garnier” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the deaths of <b>Francis Garnier</b> in 1873 and Henri Rivière in 1883 in Tonkin, the Harmand and Patenôtre treaties of 1883–1884 made central and northern Vietnam the protectorates of Annam and Tonkin under the Nguyen emperors, and the Sino-French War of 1884–1885 forced China to renounce its…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Saint-Étienne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.43,4.39&amp;year=1861" aria-label="Saint-Étienne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Francis Garnier"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5263 6224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5263 6224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.43,4.39&amp;year=1861">What was here in 1861? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
